.TH RADIO 3
.SH NAME
radio \- packet radios, transceivers and transmitters
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind -a '#W' /dev
.PP
.B /dev/radio0/data
.br
.B /dev/radio0/pkt
.br
.B /dev/radio0/ctl
.br
.B /dev/radio0/stats
.PP
.B FT891PORT=\fIdevice\fB emu
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I radio
device serves a directory per radio interface, each holding
.B ctl
and
.BR stats ,
and
.B data
and
.B pkt
as well when the radio carries packets.
.PP
Three drivers answer it.
.B Sx127x
is the Semtech SX127x family in LoRa mode, the part an HopeRF RFM9x
module is built around;
.B cc1120
is a TI CC1120 narrowband transceiver, which is what a MikroElektronika
ccRF click carries; both are reached over SPI.
.B Ft891
is a Yaesu FT-891 over a serial port, speaking the set's CAT protocol,
and has no packets at all.  Which files a directory holds follows from
that and not from a flag: a driver declares how much one packet carries,
and a driver that declares zero gets no
.B data
and no
.BR pkt .
An FT-891 has a microphone socket, not a payload, and
.I lora (3),
.I ax25 (3)
and
.I kiss (3)
attach to
.B data
by file descriptor and so cannot run over one.  That is the correct
answer and not a limitation to be worked around.
.PP
Everything from here to
.B "RADIOS WITH NO PACKETS"
is the packet case.  This is the frame layer and nothing above it.  It carries atomic
packets, the radio's configuration, and what was known about a packet
when it arrived.  It has no addresses, no ports, no acknowledgement, no
retry, no routing and no fragmentation; those belong to whatever speaks
through it, the way IP belongs above
.I ether (3)
rather than inside it.  A packet written here goes out exactly as
written and a packet read here is exactly what the modem demodulated.
.PP
.B Data
is message-delimited.  One write is one packet on the air; one read is
one packet received, blocking until there is one.  It is not a byte
stream, because a radio is not one and because what is layered above
depends on the boundary surviving:
.I ssl (3)
is a filter over a file descriptor, and Styx is a request and a reply.
.PP
.B Pkt
is
.B data
with its ancillary information, and exists because a packet radio has
per-packet facts that a stream has nowhere to put.  A read returns
twelve bytes of
.PP
.EX
	len[2] rssi[2] snr[2] flags[2] tick[4]
.EE
.PP
little-endian, with
.B rssi
and
.B snr
signed, followed by the payload.  The signal strength is in dBm and the
signal-to-noise ratio in dB, both measured on that packet rather than on
whatever arrived most recently;
.B tick
is the kernel tick it landed on; the only flag defined is
.B 1
for a packet whose CRC did not check.
.PP
Header and payload come out of one read on purpose.  A second file would
race: two readers, or one reader and two packets, could pair the wrong
header with the wrong payload, and the pairing is the whole value of the
header.  This is the shape
.I recvmsg (2)
uses on a system that has one, for the same reason.  A reader that does
not care about any of it opens
.B data
instead and the header is skipped for it; the two files read the same
queue, so a packet is delivered to whichever asks first and once.
.PP
A packet whose CRC failed is queued with the flag set rather than
discarded, because a receiver counting them is measuring its link.
.PP
.B Data
and
.B pkt
are one queue and therefore one claim.  Opening either for reading
takes the radio's receive path, and a second open for reading fails
naming what has it; a second open for writing does not, because
transmitting is serialised in the part and two processes may do it.
Without that a program opening
.B data
after
.I lora (3)
had attached would block for ever on packets that had already gone to
somebody else, which is the one failure this device is least able to
explain.
.PP
Two things are refused rather than accommodated.
.TP
.B "a short read"
A read whose buffer is smaller than the waiting packet fails, naming the
size the packet needs, and the packet stays queued for a read that fits.
It is not truncated.
.I Ssl (3)
states the contract this inherits \- as long as reads specify buffers of
equal or greater length than the writes at the other end, one write
corresponds to one read \- and a device that quietly returned the first
.I n
bytes would break it somewhere else entirely.
.TP
.B "an oversize write"
A write longer than the payload budget fails and names the budget.  It
is not split across two packets.  Splitting is a segmentation layer, and
a device that did it silently would hide the question of what message
size a link can carry.
.PP
The
.B iounit
of
.B data
is that budget, which is 255 bytes, the whole of the part's FIFO,
because this device adds nothing to what is written.  That matters
beyond documentation, because
.I exportfs
adopts a transport's iounit when negotiating a Styx message size, so a
server over this settles on a radio-sized message without being told.
The iounit of
.B pkt
is twelve more.
.PP
.B Ctl
takes one command per write:
.TP
.BI freq " hz"
Carrier frequency.
.TP
.BI power " dbm"
Transmit power, 2 to 17, through the PA_BOOST output an RFM9x bonds out.
.TP
.BI sf " n"
Spreading factor, 6 to 12.
.TP
.BI bw " hz"
Bandwidth; one of the values the part has, 7800 through 500000.
.TP
.BI cr " n"
Coding rate denominator, 5 to 8.
.TP
.BI carrier " on|off"
Listen before transmitting.  Off by default.
.TP
.BI duty " percent"
The share of the air this radio may use, 0 to 100.  Zero is off and is
the default.
.TP
.B reset
Put the part back in sleep and reapply the settings.
.PP
Those are the modem's parameters and the band's, and nothing else,
which is the point: there is no
.B node
or
.B dest
here because there is no address here.
.PP
.B Duty
is the one control that is not about the radio.  An
.B iounit
says how much one operation carries and nothing about how often, and a
.B data
file that took writes as fast as a program made them would be telling
that program something untrue: the packets go out, and what breaks is
the licence.  So the write waits, which is the only way a file
interface says this.
.PP
The rule is the one every LoRaWAN stack implements: a packet of
.I A
milliseconds on the air, at a duty cycle of
.I D
per cent, is followed by
.I "A (100 - D) / D"
of silence, so the ratio holds over every window rather than over an
hour.  At 1 per cent and SF7, a nine-byte payload is 47ms of air and
4653ms of silence.  Measured, four such writes in a row:
.PP
.EX
	duty off        240 ms
	duty 1        14190 ms
.EE
.PP
against 14147 predicted, and
.B airtime-total-ms
agreeing at 47ms a packet.  The air is charged for whether or not
TxDone arrives, because the energy went out either way and a regulator
counts transmissions rather than successes.
.PP
The first packet after a silence does not wait, so a short burst is
slightly over the ratio and a long run converges on it.  That is the
behaviour of the rule and not an approximation of it; a scheme that
made the first packet wait would be refusing to transmit into an hour
it had not used.
.PP
.B Ctl 's
.B sf
also sets LowDataRateOptimize, which the datasheet (4.1.1.6) requires
whenever a symbol lasts longer than 16ms \- SF11 and SF12 at 125kHz.
It is mandatory rather than advisory, because the receiver has to agree:
a link with one end setting it and the other not does not work.
.PP
A transmit waits for the time the packet will really be on the air,
computed from the spreading factor, bandwidth, coding rate and length,
and not for a constant.  At 125kHz a 20-byte packet is 57ms at SF7 and
1319ms at SF12, so a constant is either wrong at one end or pointlessly
long at the other.
.PP
.B Stats
returns the parameters in force, the RSSI and SNR of the last packet
received, and counters for packets sent and received
.RB ( sent ,
.BR received ),
packets that arrived with a bad CRC
.RB ( crcerrors ),
writes refused for being past the payload
.RB ( toobig ),
transmits that never reported done
.RB ( txtimeout ),
the computed airtime of a 20-byte packet in the current configuration
.RB ( airtime-20B-ms ),
the duty cycle in force
.RB ( duty ),
all the air this radio has used since boot
.RB ( airtime-total-ms ),
how long until it may transmit again
.RB ( airtime-owed-ms )
and how many writes have had to wait for that
.RB ( dutywaits ),
whether carrier sense is on
.RB ( carrier ),
transmits that found the air busy
.RB ( busywaits )
and those that went out over it after eight tries
.RB ( sentanyway ).
.PP
The part behind
.B #W
is reached through a table of operations it fills in \- send, ctl,
stat, and how much one packet carries \- and the device dereferences
nothing else of it.  A second kind of part is a second driver and no
change here; the layers above,
.I lora (3)
and
.I ax25 (3),
attach to a packet device by file descriptor and never knew which part
it was.
.SH "RADIOS WITH NO PACKETS"
An HF transceiver is a radio and is not a packet device.  It has a
frequency, a mode, a transmit key and a meter, and its audio goes to a
socket on the back panel rather than through any file here.  The
question this device was made to answer is whether
.B #W 's
idea of a radio was a radio's or one chip's, and the answer is that it
needed one change: a driver that carries no packets serves no
.B data
and no
.BR pkt .
.PP
For such a radio
.B ctl
takes:
.TP
.BI freq " hz"
Frequency, in hertz, as an integer.
.TP
.BI mode " name"
One of the modes the set has.
.B Ctl 's
read lists them.
.TP
.BI power " dbm"
Transmit power.  The vocabulary here is dBm because that is what the
rest of
.B #W
uses; a set whose own control is in watts converts, coarsely and on
purpose \- an HF power control is a knob, not a calibration.
.TP
.BI ptt " on|off"
Key and unkey the transmitter.
.TP
.B rx
.TP
.B idle
Stop transmitting.
.TP
.BI cat " string"
Send one CAT command literally and print what comes back.  The bench
door, as
.B reg
is on a chip driver: a set with a hundred commands should not need a
kernel change to answer one of them.
.PP
A serial-attached radio also sits on a port, and a port has settings
that are the transport's and not the radio's.  A command the radio does
not recognise is offered to the port before it is an error, so
.TP
.BI port " device"
.TP
.BI rate " baud"
reach the host serial layer.  This is why
.B ctl
has to exist before the radio is known to be there: it is how you say
where to look.
.PP
A read of
.B ctl
returns what may be written to it, in the syntax that would be written,
which is the arrangement
.I vga (3)
and
.I audio (3)
use and is how a program asks what a radio can do without a table of
models compiled into it.  From an FT-891:
.PP
.EX
	freq 14074000
	mode data-u
	ptt off
	power 100
	band rx 30000 56000000
	band tx 1800000 2000000 37 50
	...
	modes lsb usb cw fm am rtty-l cw-r data-l
	  rtty-u data-fm fm-n data-u am-n c4fm
	levels strength rfpower
.EE
.PP
.B Band
lines are receive and transmit ranges in hertz, transmit with the power
limits in dBm.
.B Modes
and
.B levels
are the vocabulary that the rest of the file uses.  The settable values
at the top are read from the set and not remembered by the driver,
because a transceiver has a knob and a driver that reports its own last
command is reporting the past; a read followed by a write of the same
text has to leave the radio where it was.
.PP
.B "What has been run."
An FT-891 on its own USB port \- a CP2105's Enhanced Com Port at 4800
8N2 \- driven from
.I emu
on a Mac.  It answers
.B ID0650;
to
.BR ID; ,
and
.PP
.EX
	; echo freq 14074000 >'#W/radio0/ctl'
	; echo mode data-u >'#W/radio0/ctl'
	; cat '#W/radio0/stats'
	part FT-891 cat
	freq 14074000
	mode data-u
	state 1
	smeter -106
	power 100
.EE
.PP
puts the set on 14.074 in DATA-U, which is FT8, and reads it back out
of the radio.  What has not been run is transmit: nothing here has
keyed that set, and
.B ptt
and
.B power
are what the manual says they are.
.SH "THE INTERRUPT A PACKET ARRIVES ON"
The CC1120 has no processor and does not interrupt; a pin of its does,
and which pin and what it is made to carry is the board's choice.  On a
mikroBUS socket there is one: a ccRF click wires GPIO3 of the four to
the host, so whatever the receive path needs has to come off that pin.
.PP
It carries PKT_SYNC_RXTX, which has two edges and they are not
interchangeable.  SWRU295E table 10: in RX it is "asserted when sync
word has been received and de-asserted at the end of the packet".  The
rising edge is the sync word, and at that instant the payload has not
been transmitted yet, let alone demodulated \- the FIFO is empty.  The
falling edge is the end of the packet, and it is the only moment at
which the packet is complete and can be read.
.PP
A board that enables the rising edge alone therefore receives
perfectly and reads nothing, which is what
.I /os/inferno/stm32f407/spistm32f4.c
did and what plan item 3 was.  It took two days to find because every
instrument said the receiver worked, and all of them were right: the
part heard the packet, found its sync word, checked its CRC and put it
in the FIFO.  Measured on silicon, ten packets sent from a second
board, read back out of the receiver's own registers:
.EX
	MARC_STATUS1  0x80   RX finished successfully
	MODEM_STATUS1 0xe1   SYNC_FOUND, against 0x61 before
	NUM_RXBYTES   0x0b   length + 8 payload + 2 status
	recv          0
.EE
.PP
.B Stats
counts the edges that were delivered, as
.BR "intr high N low N" ,
split by the level of the pin that raised them and counted before
anything is asked of the part.  High is a sync word, with nothing
complete to read; low is an end of packet, with something.  They exist
because
.B recv
cannot answer the question that matters when it reads zero:
.B recv
is downstream of the interrupt arriving at all, so it cannot
distinguish a receiver that heard nothing from one that heard
everything and was never told.  The two counters are upstream of it.
What the fix looks like in them, same two boards and the same ten
packets:
.EX
	recv 10  crcerr 0  rxoverflow 0  last -41dBm
	intr high 10 low 10
	lastrx 8 bytes: 61 62 63 64 65 66 67 68
.EE
.PP
Ten sync words and ten ends of packet, where the ends of packet had
been zero, and the payload is what
.B txtest
8 sends.
.PP
The negative control is worth as much.  With the receiver left on its
boot default of 868MHz while the other board transmitted on 915, both
counters stayed at zero: they do not count noise.
.PP
And the reason no emulated test could have caught this.  The qemu model
raises the pin once the packet is in its FIFO and never lowers it, so
there the rising edge does carry data and
.I tools/radio/twoboards.sh
passes either way.  A model is only ever a model of what somebody
thought the part did.
.SH "LOOKING AT A BAND"
A receiver is an instrument, and this one will say what is on the air
around it.  These are the CC1120 driver's, on
.BR ctl ,
and every one of them has a resolution limit that is worth knowing
before its numbers are believed.
.TP
.BI rate " sym/s"
.TP
.BI deviation " hz"
.TP
.BI bw " hz"
.TP
.BI sync " word [bits]"
The modem.  These decide what the receiver is listening
.I for
and nothing works until they match the transmitter.  What the part
holds at boot is TI's example table, which is 1200 symbols a second
with 3997Hz deviation in 25kHz \- a demonstration and not a choice \-
and each of these reads back from the part rather than from what the
driver meant to set.
.TP
.BI scan " lo hi step ms"
Tune each step in turn and report the peak, mean and floor of the RSSI
over
.I ms
at each.  One look per step, so for a signal that is on a few per cent
of the time this measures when the look happened rather than where the
energy is.
.TP
.BI hold " lo hi step ms passes"
The same sweep repeated, keeping the highest each step ever saw.  What
.B scan
cannot do: every step gets enough looks to catch a burst.
.TP
.BI probe " hz [ms]"
One channel, characterised.  It measures the floor and the peak, sets
carrier sense between them, and reports the duty cycle, how many
bursts, and the shortest and longest burst and gap.
.TP
.BI watch " secs"
Stay where the part is tuned and print a line per dwell.
.TP
.BI capture " ms [dbm]"
The envelope, by sampling RSSI over the bus and printing the run
lengths above and below a threshold.
.TP
.BI gcapture " ms [iocfg3]"
The same, off one of the part's own GPIO pins, which costs no bus
traffic.
.TP
.BI ghist " ms [iocfg3]"
The pin's run lengths as a histogram over the whole window rather than
a sequence.
.TP
.BI gtrig " ms iocfg3"
Wait for carrier sense, then record the pin through the burst.
.TP
.BI gsweep " [ms]"
Set IOCFG3 to each of the sixty-four selections in turn and say how
many times the pin changed.  Which value carries which signal is a
question for the part and not for a table: 0x11 is the carrier sense
here, found this way.
.TP
.BI serial " on|off"
Put the demodulated bit stream on GPIO3, or take it off again.
.B PKT_CFG2 's
PKT_FORMAT of 11 is the part's transparent serial mode, in which the
packet engine is out of the path and the slicer's output goes straight
to the pin: no sync word has to match and no CRC has to check, so a
transmission this radio cannot decode is still one it can measure.
The receiver is left in RX either way.
.PP
This is the one measurement here whose answer is a property of what is
transmitting rather than of what is listening.  A preamble is an
alternating pattern at the symbol rate, so under
.B ghist
it arrives as a pile of runs all one length, and that length is the
symbol period: 1953us is 512 symbols a second, 833us is 1200, 625us is
1600 and 417us is 2400.  Everything else on this page reports a threshold
being crossed, and a threshold is the receiver's.
.PP
"Noise has no such pile" was also written here and is not true of this
bench: measured on silicon, the pin with the mode on and nothing
transmitting gives 1613 runs in two seconds piling into the same
512-sample bin as a keyed random-data carrier does.  A slicer with no
squelch has to put out something.  What the mode gives is a bit stream
whose run lengths can be read; separating that stream from noise is a
separate problem and this page does not have an answer to it.
.PP
It now works on silicon, and the reason it had not is a third register.
.B MDMCFG1 's
FIFO_EN is 1 at reset, and SWRU295E says 1 is "data in/out through the
FIFOs" where 0 is "data in/out through the serial pin(s) (the FIFOs are
bypassed)".  This driver never wrote the register, so the demodulator's
output went where it always goes and GPIO3 had nothing to carry however
.B PKT_CFG2
and
.B MDMCFG0
were programmed.
.B "serial on"
clears it and
.B "serial off"
puts it back, and the restore matters as much: with FIFO_EN clear nothing
reaches the RX FIFO at all.
.PP
Measured, with the three registers read back off the part each time:
.PP
.EX
	mode off            0.0% high, the pin parked
	mode on, quiet      49.0 to 52.5% high, 1613 runs in 2s
	mode on, keyed      46.8 to 47.4% high, 1206 runs in 2s
.EE
.PP
So the pin moves, where before it did not move at all: the earlier
account of this said half a second and 405000 samples at one level, at
all four TRANSPARENT_INTFACT settings and with SERIAL_CLK tried on IOCFG2
and on IOCFG1 with SERIAL_RX on IOCFG0 as well, and concluded that what
the board could not rule out was the pin.
.B "That is retracted \- the pin was never the problem."
.PP
.B "And the mode is not a signal detector here, which is what it was wanted for."
The slicer has no squelch, so with nothing arriving it slices noise into
roughly random bits at roughly half duty, which is what it does with a
random-data carrier too \- and the run lengths pile up in the same bin
either way, 512 samples of 1.2us, which is 610 to 632 microseconds
against the 833 a 1200-symbol second would give.  The keyed set is
tighter (46.8 to 47.4 against 49.0 to 52.5) and has fewer and longer runs
(1206 against 1613), which is the shape real data would have, but four
readings four points apart is a hint and not a detector.
.PP
.B "One trap in measuring it, because it cost a wrong conclusion here."
The first
.B gread
after
.B "serial on"
can read 0.0 per cent.  One run of
.I tools/radio/serialsilicon.py
gave 0.0 quiet against 52.2 keyed, which looks like a clean detection,
and the next gave 43.1 against 46.6.  The 0.0 is settling and not
silence, and it is a reading that exactly equals a degenerate baseline \-
which is as likely to mean the instrument was not ready as that the
channel was empty.  That script now alternates the keyed and unkeyed
cases four times for exactly this reason.
.PP
The control is the sweep.  All sixty-four IOCFG3 selections with the
mode off and with it on: exactly three move either way and by the same
amount both times \- 0x28 at 353 and 239 runs, 0x2e at 3011 and 4350,
0x31 at 38424 and 38375.  The instrument works and the mode changes
nothing.  None of those three is the bit stream either: 0x2e and 0x31
run in ones and twos of a sample and read identically at 1200 and 4800
symbols a second, which is a clock; 0x28 pulses for one sample with
gaps of 32 to 2048 samples whose distribution moves with the symbol
rate, but
.I upward
as the rate rises, which a symbol period does not do.
.PP
What this board cannot rule out is the pin.  It wires one of the part's
four GPIOs, GPIO3 to PE14, and a mode delivering on another pin would
look exactly like this.
.PP
The method is demonstrated under qemu, by
.IR tools/radio/serialrate.sh .
The model's
.B airrate
property puts a rate on the channel that the guest was never told, and
with the guest configured for 2400 throughout, 300 symbols a second
measures a symbol period of 20 to 21.5ms against 1200's 7.3 to 7.4 \- a
ratio of 2.7 to 2.9 where the rates differ by four, on three runs.
.PP
Shown to be able to fail, which is the part that makes those three
runs worth anything.  With both rates set to 300 the same test
measures 20.7 and 21.1ms and prints SERIAL-FAILED at 0.9 \- so it is
comparing the channel and not something that would agree whatever the
channel did.  Those two numbers are also the measurement's
repeatability, 1.5 per cent.
.PP
That the ratio is 2.9 and not 4.0 is the histogram's resolution and
has to be read as such.  Its bins are a power of two apart, so it
locates a period to within a factor of two and a ratio of four to
within a factor of two either side; the test's band is set there and
not tighter.  It also takes the run-count-weighted mean of the whole
distribution rather than the fullest bin, because a period near a bin
boundary falls either side of it from one window to the next \- the
first cut read 2048 samples on one run and 4096 on the next at the
same rate.
.PP
Two things there are the model's rather than the method's.  Every
absolute value is some six times long, and 4800 symbols a second
measures the same period as 1200, both because a guest polling a pin
in a tight loop and QEMU's virtual clock do not agree.  On silicon the
sample period is 1.37us against the model's nine and the bits come
from the air rather than from a virtual timer.  What silicon has not
yet shown is a pile of any length.
.PP
.B "What each of these can resolve, and why it matters."
Three separate measurements in one day looked like a transmitter's
signature and were the receiver.
.PP
.B Capture
and
.B probe
read the envelope through the RSSI, and the shortest thing carrier
sense will assert for is one RSSI update.  That is
.B AGC_CFG0 's
RSSI_VALID_CNT estimates, and an estimate is 0.309ms on this board \-
measured four ways, the shortest burst coming out 0.608, 0.927, 1.568
and 2.79ms at 2, 3, 5 and 9 estimates.  So every duration these report
is a small multiple of 0.62ms at best, and six bursts measuring 1341
to 1343 microseconds is two resolution cells and not a frame.
.PP
.B Capture
also has its own bus in the measurement.  Polling RSSI continuously
raises this part's floor 15 to 20dB \- at 893MHz, where watch reports
a floor of \-118dBm, a capture crosses \-106 after three milliseconds
and stays there \- so its levels are not levels and its threshold has
to be chosen against a capture of a quiet channel rather than against
what watch says.
.B Gcapture
exists because of that and touches no bus at all.
.PP
.B Probe
says NOISE when its own threshold is the thing it measured.  The first
pass only catches a burst if one happens while it is looking, and when
it does not, the threshold sits near the floor and the AGC breathing
across it is counted: three runs at 918MHz within a minute reported
the channel 89 per cent busy, then 0, then 0.  Comparing the peak
against the floor does not separate those \- the bad runs had 14 and
20dB between them \- but the shape of the result does, because noise
crossing a line gives bursts and gaps of the same shortest length in
numbers no transmitter sends.
.PP
And the pin itself quantises.
.B Gtrig
at 918MHz records a burst as pulses separated by 22, 90, 158, 226 and
430 samples, varying through the burst, which reads as data; the same
capture at 926MHz gives the same set of lengths, every one a multiple
of 9.94 microseconds.  A quantum common to two channels is a clock in
the receiver.
.PP
The rule that caught all three is one command: take the identical
measurement somewhere the signal is not, and see whether the structure
survives.
